<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Trickplayer watch face — Watch Face Format, version 2.

  There is no code here and there cannot be: WFF requires hasCode="false" and
  Google requires the watch face bundle be entirely separate from the bundle
  holding app logic. Everything this face shows arrives through the complication
  API from :app.

  Interactive bands, top to bottom, on the 450x450 design canvas:

      clock     y   6 ..  50     digital, hh:mm — no seconds shown
      frame     y  54 .. 198     PHOTO_IMAGE, 256x144 — 16:9
      subtitle  y 202 .. 396     LONG_TEXT, 280 wide, 32px, 4 lines of 16 chars

  MEASURED, on a Pixel Watch 3, with a character ruler pushed through the
  subtitle complication — not calculated:

      * A 32px line occupies **48px** of height. Not 32, not 38: the runtime's
        line spacing is 1.5x and there is no attribute to change it.
      * A 280-wide band fits **16 characters** per line.

  Those two numbers decide the whole layout, and the second one decides it more
  than anything else here, because band width is capped by the circle: the chord
  at y=396 is 292, so 280 is already within a few pixels of the widest a band
  ending there can be.

      y=  6   103        y=240   449
      y= 20   185        y=380   326
      y= 52   288        y=396   292
      y= 60   306        y=420   224

  **Moving things up is not what bought the fourth line — shrinking the picture
  was.** Raising the clock to y=6 and the frame with it frees 28px, and a line
  costs 48, so the move alone gains nothing you can read. The fourth line needs
  a 192px band, which means everything above it must end by y=204, which caps a
  16:9 frame at 256x144. That is the real trade and there is no arrangement that
  avoids it: 341x192 and three lines, or 256x144 and four.

  A side effect worth noting: at 256 wide the frame is now narrower than the
  chord everywhere it sits, so nothing is clipped by the bezel any more.

  How this arrived here, because the same mistake is easy to repeat: an early
  attempt used a 360-wide band running to y=426, which wrapped correctly and
  then ran both outer lines off the bezel, with ellipsis="TRUE" doing nothing to
  stop it. A band is a rectangle and a face is a circle; the width it can use is
  the chord at its LOWEST point, not its widest.

  The text was also unreadable at arm's length at 17px, which is why it is 32.
  Every reduction in the picture since has been paid to that decision: 400x225
  bought three lines, 256x144 buys four. There is no arrangement where both the
  picture and legible subtitles get what they want, and the subtitles keep
  winning because a picture with no readable line under it is decoration.

  AMBIENT
  =======
  In ambient the face is a clock and nothing else. The picture is the whole
  point of this watch face and it is also the one thing that must not be on
  screen for hours at a time: PHOTO_IMAGE is explicitly not recommended in
  ambient, a photograph is close to the worst case for OLED burn-in, and a
  bright 400x225 block is most of the panel lit continuously.

  WFF has no if/else for this. Every element carries an `alpha` and a
  `<Variant mode="AMBIENT" target="alpha">` giving the value that alpha takes in
  ambient, and the runtime interpolates between them. Anything that cannot be
  expressed as an interpolated property — a different font weight, say — needs
  two elements cross-faded, which is why the clock appears twice below. That is
  the pattern Google's own SimpleDigital sample uses.

  The ambient clock is deliberately not the interactive one moved: it is
  centred, larger, and thin-weight, so far fewer pixels are lit.
-->
<WatchFace width="450" height="450" clipShape="CIRCLE">
    <Metadata key="CLOCK_TYPE" value="DIGITAL" />
    <Metadata key="PREVIEW_TIME" value="10:08:32" />

    <Scene backgroundColor="#000000">

        <!-- ==================== clock, interactive ====================
             As high as it goes: at y=6 the chord is 103px and "10:08" at 38px
             bold is narrower than that. Every pixel saved here goes to the
             subtitle, which is the band that runs out of room. -->
        <DigitalClock x="0" y="6" width="450" height="44">
            <TimeText
                x="0" y="0" width="450" height="44"
                format="hh:mm"
                hourFormat="SYNC_TO_DEVICE"
                align="CENTER"
                alpha="255">
                <Variant mode="AMBIENT" target="alpha" value="0" />
                <Font family="SYNC_TO_DEVICE" size="38" color="#FFFFFF" weight="BOLD" />
            </TimeText>
        </DigitalClock>

        <!-- ==================== the seconds ticker ====================
             DO NOT REMOVE THIS BECAUSE YOU CANNOT SEE IT. It is invisible on
             purpose and it is load-bearing.

             A complication timeline is only re-evaluated when the face
             redraws, and a face showing hh:mm has no reason to redraw more
             than once a minute — so timeline entries finer than a minute never
             appear and an autoplay burst of 2-second steps does not play at
             all. What drives the redraw is a clock element whose FORMAT has
             seconds granularity. The seconds are still ticking here; they are
             simply not on the face, because a running seconds counter is a
             distraction on something you glance at and it was never there for
             the user.

             **It is black text, not transparent text, and that is the whole
             trick.** MEASURED, because the obvious version does not work:

               alpha="0"              burst does NOT play. The face rendered
                                      the first entry of a 30-second timeline
                                      and sat on it through eight samples.
               color="#000000"        burst plays. Six consecutive samples,
               alpha="255"            six different frames.

             So WFF decides its update rate from what is VISIBLE, not from what
             is present in the scene — an element at zero alpha is not merely
             hidden, it is not a reason to redraw. Painting the text in the
             background colour keeps it a reason.

             Ambient is unaffected: alpha goes to 0 in the AMBIENT variant and
             the ambient clock carries no seconds, so the per-second redraw
             stops when the wrist drops, exactly as before. That the ambient
             case works *because* alpha=0 kills the redraw is the same fact
             read the other way round. -->
        <DigitalClock x="0" y="432" width="450" height="16">
            <TimeText
                x="0" y="0" width="450" height="16"
                format="ss"
                hourFormat="SYNC_TO_DEVICE"
                align="CENTER"
                alpha="255">
                <Variant mode="AMBIENT" target="alpha" value="0" />
                <Font family="SYNC_TO_DEVICE" size="12" color="#000000" />
            </TimeText>
        </DigitalClock>

        <!-- ==================== clock, ambient ====================
             A second clock rather than a restyled first one: font weight is not
             an interpolatable property, so the two cross-fade on alpha. -->
        <DigitalClock x="0" y="165" width="450" height="120">
            <TimeText
                x="0" y="0" width="450" height="120"
                format="hh:mm"
                hourFormat="SYNC_TO_DEVICE"
                align="CENTER"
                alpha="0">
                <Variant mode="AMBIENT" target="alpha" value="255" />
                <Font family="SYNC_TO_DEVICE" size="88" color="#FFFFFF" weight="LIGHT" />
            </TimeText>
        </DigitalClock>

        <!-- Seconds are in the interactive clock and NOT in the ambient one,
             which is the whole reason there are two clocks here.

             A complication timeline is only re-evaluated when the face redraws,
             and a face showing hh:mm redraws once a minute — so without seconds
             a burst of short entries never plays. This face is glanced at for
             the length of a screen timeout and should show a run of the show in
             that window, so per-second redraws are the feature.

             On the EMULATOR this appears to cost the ambient variant: the
             runtime seems to stop applying AMBIENT and dim the interactive face
             instead. On real hardware (Pixel Watch 3, Wear OS / API 37) it does
             not — left alone, the watch settles into the proper ambient variant
             with the minute-only clock and no picture.

             The emulator measurement was also partly self-inflicted: taking a
             screenshot over adb requires interacting with the watch, and that
             interaction is what kept it out of ambient. Do not trust an ambient
             measurement taken while something is poking the device. -->

        <!-- ==================== frame ====================
             Hidden outright in ambient — burn-in and power. -->
        <ComplicationSlot
            slotId="1"
            displayName="Trickplayer scene"
            supportedTypes="PHOTO_IMAGE SMALL_IMAGE EMPTY"
            x="97" y="54" width="256" height="144"
            alpha="255">

            <BoundingBox x="0" y="0" width="256" height="144" />

            <Complication type="PHOTO_IMAGE">
                <PartImage x="0" y="0" width="256" height="144">
                    <Image resource="[COMPLICATION.PHOTO_IMAGE]" />
                </PartImage>
            </Complication>

            <Complication type="SMALL_IMAGE">
                <PartImage x="0" y="0" width="256" height="144">
                    <Image resource="[COMPLICATION.SMALL_IMAGE]" />
                </PartImage>
            </Complication>

            <Variant mode="AMBIENT" target="alpha" value="0" />
        </ComplicationSlot>

        <!-- ==================== subtitle ====================
             Also hidden. Static text held for hours is its own burn-in risk,
             and a subtitle with no frame above it says nothing anyway. -->
        <ComplicationSlot
            slotId="2"
            displayName="Trickplayer subtitle"
            supportedTypes="LONG_TEXT SHORT_TEXT EMPTY"
            x="85" y="202" width="280" height="194"
            alpha="255">

            <BoundingBox x="0" y="0" width="280" height="194" />

            <Complication type="LONG_TEXT">
                <PartText x="0" y="0" width="280" height="194">
                    <Text align="CENTER" maxLines="4" ellipsis="TRUE">
                        <Font family="SYNC_TO_DEVICE" size="32" color="#FFFFFF">
                            <Template><![CDATA[%s]]><Parameter expression="[COMPLICATION.TEXT]" /></Template>
                        </Font>
                    </Text>
                </PartText>
            </Complication>

            <Complication type="SHORT_TEXT">
                <PartText x="0" y="0" width="280" height="194">
                    <Text align="CENTER" maxLines="2" ellipsis="TRUE">
                        <Font family="SYNC_TO_DEVICE" size="32" color="#FFFFFF">
                            <Template><![CDATA[%s]]><Parameter expression="[COMPLICATION.TEXT]" /></Template>
                        </Font>
                    </Text>
                </PartText>
            </Complication>

            <Variant mode="AMBIENT" target="alpha" value="0" />
        </ComplicationSlot>

    </Scene>
</WatchFace>
